   "file": "projects.md",
   "heading": "Dual-Modality Pulmonary Imaging for Bedside Monitoring Applications",
   "text": "Dual-Modality Pulmonary Imaging for Bedside Monitoring Applications. My master's dissertation explored the use of amplitude and phase data from a wearable sensor system for pulmonary monitoring. I initially used MATLAB to store and analyse the data, then developed a machine-learning pipeline to investigate whether phase information could be enhanced using amplitude-derived information.\n\nI applied a Hilbert transform for phase enhancement, extracted features from the signals and trained Random Forest, Logistic Regression and k-Nearest Neighbours classifiers to distinguish three breathing states. I evaluated the models using accuracy, precision, recall and F1-score, with four-fold cross-validation.\n\nThis project combined mathematical signal processing with machine learning. It taught me to investigate the quality of the input data, compare different approaches and evaluate model performance systematically.\n\nStack: MATLAB, Python, NumPy, SciPy, scikit-learn, signal processing, feature engineering.",
